     "result_text_tail": "went ahead with the storyline as I would have recommended it.\n- **Fiscal years:** I read FY2026 as the year that starts after the February 2027 meeting, which makes it the covenant year. That is the only reading under which the pack's dates fit together, and it is stated in a footnote.\n- **Store exits:** the pack only gives totals for the nine weak stores, so I split them between the six and the three by store count.\n- **Shrink effect of the hour cut:** I put all of the FY2023\u201325 rise in shrink down to thinner staffing. That makes the $10M of extra shrink an upper bound, and the slide says so.\n- **Private label:** I assumed it keeps its FY2023\u201325 pace of 0.75 percentage points of sales a year through FY2028.\n\n**Risks the board should hear:** the plan clears the 3.0% target by only about $1.5M. A 4% wage rise across the whole store-labor bill would cost up to $7.3M, and the 2027 minimum-wage rise is 4%. Without the three long-lease store exits, the margin reaches about 2.9%. Two possible upsides are not counted: vendor funding returning toward 55% of markdowns, and a lower delivery rate at the contract renewal.\n\nNext, I could add a backup slide that tests the plan against the wage rise, or switch the theme.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- `harlow-margin-plan.pptx`\n- `harlow-margin-plan-sources.xlsx` (one sheet per content slide, plus a Calculations sheet with live formulas)\n- `harlow-margin-plan.json` (the spec the deck is built from; regenerate it with `work/build_spec.py`)"
    }
